92186505712 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505712 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505712 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505712 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505712 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505712 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505712 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186505597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505712 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d848507f-dcf4-5e8d-8a83-e80e1621fe7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505717 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505717 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505717 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505717 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505717 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505717 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505717 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505717 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186505471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505717 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e3ad2a19-9ee2-55dd-b140-b09c354ee276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505730 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505730 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505730 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505730 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/74/74a33fb06f8ef0f2f9f7935f5c53209392f3289e20be803b9d82737a46b31c06.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505730 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0028aa3d-01ab-5dd0-b507-a0e026d36abf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505730 #edb/ref 1074 "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=g(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=g(x)$ is concave down in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ and $y={g}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c16ffbba0310799c36ee1f21359b96f93f73ad121b7d5770784fbf6033ca0446.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505740 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505740 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505740 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505740 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c1/c1de00f79b0621d8b9f5f5731f4796bd53d3319451deca953bc12cfb4c24f65d.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505740 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15879841-b72a-52ec-bd4a-969c8835b3a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505740 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from negative to positive) when we pass through $x=3$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=3$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/17/17ccf1c0863b25fd8cb0d4227c43c071e3e24f35831ae1eed6c262aca353ba08.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505749 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505749 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114643" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505749 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505749 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d6/d69caa35281c084636fbbf530b40c264ad4cdd7e626f941be67d3c0e0405a4ba.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505749 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b0e787a-88a1-5ceb-86de-c3a98e9d2857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505749 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is constant, we get ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $\\text{const.}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $0$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from positive to  $0$) when we pass through $x=3$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=3$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/88f858ca236aabbf86103dbd6a5227821e7b2bf9611dde0872a6a1e34bc6b931.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505750 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505750 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505750 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505750 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0f/0f03e226c0d65852c48ef5136131db98b563da6885cb9930f6227e7908aba584.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505750 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c0c91e8-a70d-5113-bcb2-8f7a22529a09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505750 #edb/ref 1074 "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the critical points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the critical points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has an inflection point at $x≈0.$ At the inflection point, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈0.$\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${g}^{′}(0)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangent to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈0$ is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a68eb6c7dc24329ad71732682f0b489e0315e058a3928db5cbbcb9182c46cab0.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505759 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505759 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505759 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505759 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/82/82edcc0447d4a2f6237c810c474e72369d8a7f1df7b75b7d1cee575dc6648f87.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Of the following, which could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505759 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c2bbcbb-181d-5929-9b37-8caf2befb3ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505759 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/6b/6b738f1d76c14e03e048ca751dbfa416f05e415ca3cf1729fef6821d7d44d00f.png)" #edb/ref 175921860