gent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈1.5.$ So ${f}^{″}(1.5)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(1.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈1.5,$ the slope is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57e2244806cb3b1168da1a1be30f923b7ee721431addd926654fcb4e51c2063b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505829 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505829 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505829 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fd/fdf32d536487a7927752daa6ea293244ca217f528881630827bc85d6db5044d2.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2c85a10-a5e4-538c-9cb8-85994d6879ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505829 #edb/ref 1074 "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the critical points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the critical points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has an inflection point at $x≈2.$ At the inflection point, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈2.$\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${g}^{′}(2)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangent to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈2$ is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below. \n\n![](images/c4/c4ffc73403729de6b9869bb70f1b466a3d426a615615e0f80d15293e97320c6b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505830 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505830 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505830 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505830 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/47/472b1c50cef2f5ce7be11fc83eda45775e734125ba0572305ab828dc7ed278b4.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Of the following, which could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505830 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3e7c579-7100-5dba-9db2-7857d6271cd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505830 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice the following: \n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{not extr.}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also:\n\n- For $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down. So, ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing on this interval.\n\n- For $x\\in (-1,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is a increasing straight line. So, $y={f}^{′}(x)$ will be a positive constant on this interval.\n\nAdditionally, we have a rapid change of the slope of the tangent line when we pass through $x=-1$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=-1,$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/1c/1ca0e089b095330fb1441325c2a71a56423df60c6cb18acade6d75f06a040ef1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505832 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505832 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79362" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505832 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505832 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c1/c11e098426544491bf77324dc380997c5883eff6ea5b589d75bd80055ecc3d83.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505832 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e93d51a5-9dcf-5b93-bb29-2d6994b5330e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505832 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1.5)$ | $-1.5$ | $(-1.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --